A||||5||Hank is Here||Clay Bars?||||1||
Z||000000||Hank is Here||02-16-2001||07:29 PM||||Has anyone used clay bars on their paint to remove deep dirt and wax?  If so what are your opinions.<BR>I looked for them today and i only found one brand at Pep Boys for like $17 with some speical clay bar lube and crappy liquid wax.  Where can I get just the clay bar and lube, I like paste wax.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||02-16-2001||07:43 PM||||I have been using the clay bar product from <A HREF="http://www.zainobros.com" TARGET=_blank>www.zainobros.com</A>  for about a year now.  All clay bars are not created equal.  You get what you pay for and good ones run about 20 bucks.  The car I used it on sat in storage for 11 years without a cover on it.  The clay bar really took the decade old environmental fall out , out of the paint.  I then followed up with the other zaino products and have been very happy.  For the lubricant I used the formula of soap and water as per their instructions on the web site.  Its important that you firs wash the car in DAWN, to cut thru the old wax before you clay it. You must also follow up after claying with some kind of protectant for your now clean/bare paint. Also, when claying watch for sand or debris in the clay or you will scratch your paint.  Constantly turn the clay over and over.  It takes a little practice, but you will soon catch on.  Follow the above web site for hints.  The guys on <A HREF="http://www.lt1.com" TARGET=_blank>www.lt1.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.edmunds.com" TARGET=_blank>www.edmunds.com</A>  car forums swear buy this stuff.  Check out those forums also to get helpful ideas on how to clay.<P>Ross<P>This is what my car looks like aftery claying and zaino <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003403.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003403.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 02-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||LZeitgeist||02-17-2001||01:56 AM||||Question... OEM Fiero paint isn't bare - it's clearcoated...<P>Does this matter when using a clay bar? I've heard good things about these, too...||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||02-17-2001||07:56 AM||||No it will not hurt.  What I meant by "bare" was clean!! Go to the web sites above and read everything you can before using. You can only hurt the paint/clear coat if you get some sand in the clay and don't turn it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ToddAsh||02-17-2001||03:51 PM||||BE CAREFUL! I put a nice scratch inthe fender of my '56 Chevy with a clay bar.Picked it up of the paint and worked like sandpaper. Cleaned and smoothed the paint but after the scratch I tossed it in the trash. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||02-19-2001||09:15 AM||||Thanks, RossT - I'll check it out...||||reg||1
